type instead of recycling smaller allocations.\n\nLet me think through the actual memory footprint here. On a 24GB GPU, the prover is already juggling traces, proving keys, and intermediate buffers across different phases. If I'm pinning 1.6GB just for Round 0 buffers—which the 2GB safety check would allow—that's eating into a significant chunk of available GPU memory before proving even starts.\n\nThe tricky part is that while these pre-allocated buffers get freed after Round 0 via cudaFreeAsync, they don't actually release the physical memory immediately. Instead, they return to the CUDA pool, which might grow to accommodate them and then hold onto that extra memory even after the buffers are gone. This could bloat the pool unnecessarily.\n\nBut wait, that still doesn't explain why GKR would actually be slower—more pool memory should make allocations faster, not slower. Maybe the issue is that buffers this large trigger a different allocation strategy in the CUDA driver, one that's less efficient for the subsequent allocation patterns GKR needs.\n\nLet me check the actual buffer sizes by looking at how the Round 0 buffer size functions compute their values, and I can add some tracing to see what's happening in practice.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":877,"cache_read_input_tokens":95312,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":877},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0bb129e0-2d55-41ce-bfd8-6ccf294e6721","uuid":"aec01a48-10ed-4cf9-b237-2e943e13a3b6"}
