e single-threaded theory at all. Maybe the safety valve is reducing to 2 or 4 threads instead of 1, and I need to trace through the logic more carefully to understand what's actually happening with memory_limit_bytes.\n\nThe real problem is that GKR's preallocation works fine because the intermediates buffer size is a fixed per-AIR constant, but Round 0's preallocation fails because it's bounded by max_temp_bytes, which can consume a huge fraction of GPU memory. I should either add logging to see the actual buffer sizes and thread counts, or just avoid pre-allocating those large intermediate buffers in Round 0 and cap the pre-allocated size to something reasonable.\n\nThe Mutex contention happens during the allocation call itself, so I can't eliminate it without pre-allocating. But instead of pre-allocating at the maximum possible size, I could pre-allocate at a typical size and fall back to per-AIR allocation when needed—this avoids the massive allocation while still covering most cases. Or even simpler: compute the exact buffer sizes needed for each AIR during work item construction and store them there, so each thread allocates only what it needs.\n\nBut wait, that's not actually the bottleneck. The real issue is the `cudaMallocAsync`/`cudaFreeAsync` calls that acquire the MemoryManager mutex and cause cross-stream synchronization. Let me reconsider—maybe the problem is that pre-allocated buffers are oversized for most AIRs, forcing kernels to process unnecessarily large ranges. Though the kernel parameters are per-AIR, so it should only process the needed portion. Actually, looking at how the intermediates buffer size is calculated, it depends on grid and block dimensions that are determined by...\n\nThe kernel launch parameters are computed inside the CUDA kernel launch function based on the AIR's specific parameters, not on the Rust side. The CUDA code determines grid and block dimensions and launches accordingly, so the pre-allocated buffer size doesn't actually affect the kernel launch parameters. The kernel knows exactly how much of the buffer to use based on those parameters, and any extra capacity in the pre-allocated buffer just sits unused.\n\nSo if the kernel behavior is identical between per-AIR and pre-allocated buffers, the regression must come from memory pressure consuming too much GPU memory, the safety valve reducing thread count and parallelism, or the extra CPU work during work item construction. I should add some debug logging to see the actual max buffer sizes and thread count with a minimal test, though I can't easily run the full benchmark with print statements. Let me reason through what the memory_limit_bytes is likely to be and trace where it's being set.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4315,"cache_read_input_tokens":99206,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4315},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0bb129e0-2d55-41ce-bfd8-6ccf294e6721","uuid":"e61c03e4-a842-473a-aadd-2afc56d4f73a"}
