{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01QXSku1KYPHTUR4j8z6GtAR","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I see `num_airs_present = ctx.per_trace.len()` at line 674. This is used for sizing arrays. The prover handles ALL AIRs in the context. So for APC 300 with 2 segments, each segment should have ~311 AIRs.\n\nBut the log shows work_items=20. Let me check if maybe the `ctx.per_trace` actually filters out AIRs with no trace (which is what `per_trace` docs say: \"For each AIR with non-empty trace\").\n\nAt APC 300 with 623 AIR instances, maybe only 20 have non-empty traces? No, that doesn't make sense - the spec shows 106,842 columns and 811M cells.\n\n\nI should add logging to capture `ctx.per_trace.len()` directly at the start of `prove_zerocheck_and_logup_gpu` to see what's actually being counted. Looking at the benchmark logs more carefully, I notice the work_items values vary across lines—20, 19, 20 again—which suggests the segments might have different numbers of active AIRs rather than a uniform distribution.\n\nCounting through the log output, I see roughly 10 segments total: 5 with around 20 work items each, then 5 with 42 work items each. This pattern could come from the APC 300 app proof having its own segments, plus multiple recursion layers (leaf, inner) and compression stages, each contributing their own segments to the total count. The real issue is that work_items never exceeds 42, so multi-threading never kicks in—we're always stuck with num_threads=1. While pre-allocating a single large buffer does eliminate repeated alloc/free cycles compared to per-AIR allocations, the CPU overhead from computing buffer sizes is apparently outweighing those gains and causing the regression. num_threads=1, pre-allocation reduces CUDA API calls from ~20×4 = ~80 per batch (per-AIR alloc/free for 4 buffer types) to 4 (one per buffer type at the start). And the max-size computation adds ~20 `compute_logup