at's happening across the board.\n\nWait, I think I found it. Adding that extra `u32` field to the `AirDataGpu` struct changes its memory layout, which could throw off cache alignment for all the other fields that get accessed constantly during proving. Since this struct is hit repeatedly for things like `interaction_rules` and `zerocheck_round0`, even a subtle alignment shift could cascade into measurable performance degradation across multiple phases.\n\nThough honestly, a single `u32` shouldn't cause a 186ms hit. Let me try a different approach instead—I'll compute the `logup_round0_buffer_size` on demand and cache it separately in the `LogupZerocheckGpu` struct rather than baking it into the proving key structure itself. Or even simpler: calculate it once when the segment is initialized and store it in a Vec so I can reference it later without modifying the core struct layout.\n\nWait, the control test showed GKR at 537ms without my changes, so something in my modifications is definitely causing the regression. Let me unstash my changes but skip the pkey.rs modification—instead I'll compute `logup_round0_buffer_size` at the start of `sumcheck_uni_round0_polys` and cache it locally. Actually, looking back at my original version before the pkey fix, that also showed GKR regression (744ms), and it didn't touch pkey.rs at all, so the pkey change isn't the culprit. Let me trace through the numbers: first version had Round 0 at 506ms and GKR at 744ms, then with the pkey fix it dropped to 442ms and 723ms respectively—so the GKR actually improved slightly.\n\nThe expensive `compute_logup_round0_buffer_size()` calls with `SymbolicConstraints::from()` are happening during Round 0 work item construction, but GKR runs before that, so CPU work in Round 0 shouldn't affect GKR timing. Unless the metrics measurement itself is off—let me check the hierarchy to see if there's something wrong with how the timing is being attributed.\n\nLooking at the metrics, the Constraints span 